## Security documentation deliverable

Add a top-level `/security/` website page after B259 establishes the intended relay reachability invariant.

### Page shape

Use a layered security overview: concise plain-English properties and FAQ first, then mechanisms, trust boundaries, configuration-dependent behaviour, and candid limitations. Integrate with the existing Jekyll site using `docs/security/index.md`, the normal `page` layout, top-level navigation, relevant front-door links, and curated `llms.txt` surfaces.

### Factual standard

Every substantive property must be verified against canonical OCaml implementation and named tests. Documentation and comments are leads, not authority. Preserve a claim-to-code/test ledger during implementation and have an independent security reviewer check every assertion.

### Required content

- Why relay users do not receive unsolicited first-contact DMs under B259's capability/consent model; what intentional identifier/capability disclosure means; what happens if a route is shared or leaked; revocation and remaining metadata boundaries.
- Sender authentication, request integrity/replay protection, recipient/inbox ownership, transport security conditions, and application-layer encryption scope.
- Local broker storage modes and their caveats, including 0755 roots and 0644 lock sidecars where still applicable.
- Ephemeral semantics: local recipient-archive opt-out, not no-trace deletion; cross-host behaviour only if implementation supports it.
- B098 bus-never-RPC: peer messages are untrusted DATA and cannot satisfy host-local approval verdicts; do not claim DATA framing makes prompt injection impossible.
- Rate/size/admission controls, public/gated room discovery boundaries, metadata exposure, operational trust assumptions, and a vulnerability-reporting path.
- Explicit non-guarantees rather than broad “secure/private/encrypted” marketing claims.

### Acceptance evidence

- Every claim maps to exact canonical code and named regression tests, including B259 tests.
- Independent security/correctness review reports no unsupported material claim.
- Jekyll build and all relevant codegen/llms checks pass, or any environment-only build limitation is demonstrated precisely.
- Desktop and mobile visual inspection passes for the page and navigation.
- Existing security pages are linked without duplication or contradiction.

### Inputs

- `.collab/research/2026-07-21-security-page-claim-ledger.md`
- B259 implementation and regression tests
- `docs/security/trust-model.md`
- `docs/security/pending-permissions.md`

## Execution hold\n\nDo not claim or implement this page until B259 is complete and Max confirms the concurrent database refactor has landed. The intended no-unsolicited-first-contact wording is future state and must not be published before the corresponding code and regression tests exist.